  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and contact information for resources available to Congressional offices. The resources include internal government agencies and libraries, such as the House and Senate websites, Legislative Branch agencies like the Library of Congress and the Congressional Research Service, and external agencies like the National Archives and the Government Publishing Office.\n\nThe House of Representatives resources include the House website, the House Library, the House Information Technology Services, and various other offices like the House Clerk and the House Administration. The Senate resources include the Senate website, the Senate Library, the Senate Information Technology Services, and various other offices like the Secretary of the Senate and the Sergeant at Arms.\n\nBoth the House and Senate have access to various Legislative Branch agencies, such as the Congressional Research Service, the Congressional Budget Office, the Government Accountability Office, and the Office of Management and Budget. External agencies like the National Archives and the Government Publishing Office also provide resources and services to Congressional offices.\n\nThe report also mentions various contact methods for each resource, including phone numbers and email addresses. Additionally, it provides links to the websites for each resource for easy access. Overall, the report serves as a comprehensive guide for Congressional offices to access various resources and services available to them.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and contact information for resources available to Congressional offices. The resources include internal government agencies and libraries, such as the House and Senate websites, Legislative Branch agencies like the Library of Congress and the Congressional Research Service, and external agencies like the National Archives and the Government Publishing Office.\n\nThe House of Representatives resources include the House website, the House Library, the House Information Technology Services, and various other offices like the House Clerk and the House Administration. The Senate resources include the Senate website, the Senate Library, the Senate Information Technology Services, and various other offices like the Secretary of the Senate and the Sergeant at Arms.\n\nBoth the House and Senate have access to various Legislative Branch agencies, such as the Congressional Research Service, the Congressional Budget Office, the Government Accountability Office, and the Office of Management and Budget. External agencies like the National Archives and the Government Publishing Office also provide resources and services to Congressional offices.\n\nThe report also mentions various contact methods for each resource, including phone numbers and email addresses. Additionally, it provides links to the websites for each resource for easy access. Overall, the report serves as a comprehensive guide for Congressional offices to access various resources and services available to them.",
  "score": 21.768706997454775,
  "answers": [
    "This report provides a brief list of key House, Senate, legislative support agencies, and executive branch offices, as well as links to online resources of use to new congressional staff who work with legislative procedures and conduct legislative research. Some of the websites listed are available only to congressional offices; other sites are restricted by chamber and are only available to those staff working in either House or Senate offices. This report is intended for congressional use only and will be updated annually."
  ],
